WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.037
In the Euclidean plane, a straight segment gives the shortest route between two points

00:00:06.037 --> 00:00:11.674
A geodesic generalizes the idea of continuing straight while constrained to a curved space

00:00:11.674 --> 00:00:15.700
Its meaning depends on the metric used to measure lengths.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.811
On a sphere, great circles supply geodesics

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:23.827
The circle shown is a cross-section through the sphere's center

00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:33.867
Along it, arc length equals radius times angle in radians, so a radius-two sphere and angle pi over three give length two pi over three.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:42.283
Traveling the other way along the same great circle reaches the same endpoint by a much longer arc

00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:47.818
That arc is still part of a geodesic, but it is not globally shortest

00:00:47.818 --> 00:00:52.167
A geodesic guarantees only local straightness, with short segments minimizing length.

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:58.020
The sphere's Gaussian curvature is one divided by radius squared

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:06.215
Do not confuse it with the ordinary curvature of a circular path, which is one divided by radius

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:12.133
The cross-section illustrates the sphere's geometry; the surface-curvature formula requires its own derivation.
